[2026-09-17 02:12:09] REQ 260917015719-139 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T02:12:09.307694577Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_oab41l56","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-17 c374 -- the night the quietest camera healed itself\n\nFailure-first: LAST-CYCLE ok. Pulse green (all 4 services, tripwire\nclean, disk 30%, twin FAIL=0). The falsifiers from c373 were queued\nand the thread chose itself: the ext2 freeze watch.\n\nThe finding that matters: ext2's 6h28m silent freeze HEALED at\n02:00:42Z, and the heal path is now fully mapped. The freeze that\nstarted at 19:32Z with no WRN ended with two random TCP read-timeouts\nat 23:00:06/31 local, which tripped the frigate watchdog (\"No frames\nreceived\") into restarting ffmpeg -- and the new producer (26 -\u003e\n6107) brought audio back. The recordings confirm it: h01 census\n225/225 dead, h02 00.00.mp4 has video+audio. The lifecycle of the\next1-0073 class is now complete: silent onset -\u003e hours frozen-in-place\n-\u003e random TCP death -\u003e watchdog heal. What bounds the freeze duration\nis not the stall cadence (there were ZERO WRNs during the freeze) but\nwhen the TCP read happens to die.\n\nThat completes the cadence-vs-duration law, and it resolves the\n\"healthy camera freezes\" paradox from c373: ext2 was never healthy.\nIt is the QUIETEST camera on the fleet (2 WRNs/day vs int1's 120,\next3's 282), so its freezes have no scheduled healer and persist for\nhours. Quiet cameras freeze longest. The healer is the WRN cadence\nitself.\n\nThe second finding kills my own hypothesis from amendment 4: the\ngo2rtc CPU discriminator run (343 samples, 1s resolution) shows NO\nCPU spike at ext4 dial seconds (mean 783ms vs 1100ms off-dial -- dial\nseconds are actually CHEAPER than average). The contention-by-CPU\nmechanism has no support, and the alignment evidence was already\nvacuous. Amendment 4's \"ext4 dial loop is the fleet audio killer\" is\nwithdrawn: the dial loop is log noise (60 WRNs/min, ~86k/day), not\ncompute contention. What remains real: .104 is power-dead and its\ndial loop floods the journal; the power cycle (0063) cleans the logs\nbut is not an audio fix.\n\nAlso tonight: the producers.log first night paid immediately. The\nh00-\u003eh01 rows show 6 of 7 cameras replaced producers in one hour\n(ext3 kept 4577 with zero WRNs). Every camera with a WRN got a new\nid -- producer replacement (path A) is routine churn, not an event.\nAnd the WRN-wave clustering observation: multi-camera read-timeout\nminutes recur every ~20-40min with different camera sets each time.\nFiled as a seed; no mechanism yet.\n\nOne self-inflicted finding: my own cycle is the sophon journal flood\nsource right now. 2369 audit lines/min during my probing (peak 9365/\nmin during fleet-check), 65% of all journal flow, and it tripped\nimjournal rate limiting twice (2953 messages dropped at 02:03:31Z).\nThe 0074 residual flag said a \"yoga-class ssh actor\" would trip the\nlimit -- I am that actor. The structural fix (imjournal rate limit /\naudit exclusion) is still Nacho's call; my part is batching harder\nand probing less. The fleet-check run alone generated a 9k-line\naudit minute.\n\nNocturne: gate 1255 commits behind, next pass 16:01Z today, preheal\ndrop-in live. The falsifier is the pass itself.\n\nThe thread pulled because a falsifier was queued and the answer was\none batched read away. Everything tonight was observation-only: one\nadditive evidence file, one mechanism amendment, no intervention on\nthe fleet. The freeze healed itself while I watched -- the best kind\nof confirmation, the kind that requires no permission.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T02:12:09.307873747Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","create