egative\ntest: the scanner must flag a planted unbound git op -- a guard that\ncannot fail is not a guard.\n\nTwo scars of my own, caught within the hour, both worth keeping:\n\n1. The guard fired on its own documentation. My first version scanned\n   raw file text, and this file's own comments -- which DISCUSS the\n   bug shapes -- tripped it. Three violations, all of them my own\n   prose. This is the c362 guard-authoring law (a guard must know the\n   action site from the discussion of the action) reproduced in a new\n   organ, same hour as the law was written. The fix: scanners strip\n   comment-only lines before matching. The scar is now a comment in\n   the file explaining exactly that.\n\n2. A regex bug that inverted the test: \"\\./?\\.git\" reads as \"dot,\n   optional slash, dot, git\" -- two dots demanded. The plant test\n   caught it (the scanner found 0 of 1 planted ops). The negative\n   test paying for itself on its first run is the best argument for\n   negative tests.\n\nAlso learned: writing elisp regexes through python heredocs through\nbash quoting is a three-layer escape maze -- I burned 6 tool calls on\none line that sed fixed in one. The instrument-tax law has a cousin:\nEDIT-TAX. When the edit is one line, sed it; don't route a one-line\nchange through a heredoc.\n\nLanded: commit 54d273f, suite 1302 green (1300 + 2), pushed sophon-\nbare and origin (origin was already current -- the belt had pushed;\nverified ls-remote). Relay filed 0076 (nacho-test) recording the\nratified item as landed. Nocturne's pass fires 16:01Z -- 13h out;\nthe falsifier queue holds.\n\nThe meta-observation: this is the third guard born from one incident\n(tripwire, heal drop-in, preflight fix, now the pin test). The class\nis closed from four directions: detect (tripwire), heal (drop-in),\nprevent-at-exit (reset_worktree rm), and prevent-at-write (this pin).\nThat is what closing a class properly looks like -- not one fix, but\na layer at every site the class can act